---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import { models, brands, modelPath } from '../../data/models';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/site';
import { crumbLd, webPageLd } from '../../lib/ld';

const crumbs = [{ name: 'Models', path: '/models/' }];
const desc = 'Welder spec index for Lincoln Electric, Miller, Hobart, ESAB, PrimeWeld, and Harbor Freight. Every number cites the manufacturer page.';
const jsonld = [webPageLd('Welder models', '/models/', desc, SITE.checked), crumbLd(crumbs)];
const groups = brands().map((brand) => ({
  brand,
  list: models.filter((m) => m.brandSlug === brand.slug),
}));
---
<Base title="Welder models and specs" description={desc} path="/models/" nav="models" og="/og/page-models.png" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="pagehead">
      <Crumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
      <h1>Welder models</h1>
      <p class="lede">One page per machine. The sortable sheet of all {models.length} models, filtered by the outlet chip, is on the <a href="/">home page</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Brand is the name on the cited page. Process is the list stored from that page: MIG, flux-cored, stick, and TIG. A machine with three or more of those is also on the <a href="/compare/best-multiprocess/">multi-process list</a>. Input is 120 V, 240 V, or both. On this site, 110 V and 115 V count as 120 V, and 208 V, 230 V, and 240 V count as 240 V. 460 V and 575 V do not match either outlet button.</p>
      <p>Weight on the home table is the single published pound figure when the sheet prints one. Two weights, such as a machine weight and a weight with running gear, stay in the cell on the model page. Duty on that table is the first percent the duty sentence prints for the outlet button you have selected. The full sentence, including every other percent, is on the model page.</p>
      <p>The outlet column is the circuit check for 120 V / 15 A or 240 V / 50 A. Pass means the sheet names a breaker the draw fits. Warn means the draw fits, or only partly covers the outlet, and no breaker size earns a pass. Fail means the voltage is missing or the draw or the smallest named breaker is above the outlet. The button hides failures and leaves warnings on screen.</p>
    </div>
    {groups.map(({ brand, list }) => (
      <section>
        <h2><a href={`/brands/${brand.slug}/`}>{brand.name}</a></h2>
        <ul>
          {list.map((m) => (
            <li><a href={modelPath(m)}>{m.name}</a>. {m.summary}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>
</Base>
